<report>
<title>Normal distribution (z) test - single sample</title>
<table>
<tr><td>Sample name:</td><td>@name</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sample mean =</td><td>@{mean:roundu}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Population mean =</td><td>@{pop_mean:roundu}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sample size n =</td><td>@size</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sample sd =</td><td>@{sd:roundu}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Population sd =</td><td>@{psd:roundu}</td></tr>
</table>

<p><ci>@{pc:round2}% confidence interval @for = @{from:roundu} to @{to:roundu}</ci></p>

<p>Standard normal deviate (z) = @{z:roundu}<br />
<pval>One sided @{p_1:pval}</pval><br />
<pval>Two sided @{p_2:pval}</pval></p>

<p>For lognormal data:<br />
<subtotal>Geometric mean = @{gmean:roundu}  (@{pc2:round2}% reference range = @{lrr:roundu} to @{urr:roundu})</subtotal></p>
<block name="warn">

<p><warn>WARNING: numbers too small for this test, consider using a Student t test instead</warn></p>
</block>
</report>
